<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marie Curie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Maria Salomea Skłodowska Curie, Maria Skłodowska</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marie Curie, born Maria Salomea Skłodowska in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, was a Polish and naturalised-French physicist and chemist. She moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1891 and with her husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a>, carried out research on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a>, a term she coined, discovering the elements polonium and radium. She shared the 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics and won the 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, the first woman to win a Nobel Prize and the only person to win it in two scientific fields. She founded the Curie Institute in Paris in 1920 and died in 1934 of aplastic anaemia, likely from exposure to radiation in her research.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marie-curie.jpg" alt="Marie Curie" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Manuel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marie_Curie_c._1920s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maria Skłodowska was born on 7 November 1867 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, the fifth and youngest child of the teachers Bronisława and Władysław Skłodowski. Both sides of the family had lost their property through involvement in Polish national uprisings. Her father taught mathematics and physics, and after the Russian authorities removed laboratory instruction from Polish schools he brought equipment home to teach his children. Her mother died of tuberculosis in 1878. She graduated from a girls' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> on 12 June 1883 with a gold medal and, unable to enrol in higher education because she was a woman, joined the clandestine Flying University with her sister Bronisława.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1891 she followed her sister Bronisława to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, enrolled at the university and lived in a garret in the Latin Quarter, studying by day and tutoring in the evening. She received a degree in physics in 1893 and a second degree in 1894. Józef Wierusz-Kowalski introduced her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a>, who found her laboratory space.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel">Henri Becquerel</a> found that uranium salts emitted rays, she took them up as a thesis subject and showed with an electrometer that their activity depended only on the quantity of uranium present. She concluded that the radiation came from the atom itself, an important step in disproving that atoms were indivisible.</p><p>At first the Nobel committee intended in 1903 to honour only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a> and Becquerel, but after the Swedish mathematician Magnus Gösta Mittag-Leffler alerted Pierre, her name was added. She married Pierre in 1895. After Pierre was killed in a road accident on 19 April 1906, she took over his chair and became the first woman professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> she developed mobile radiography units, popularly called petites Curies, and directed the installation of 20 of them and 200 radiological units at field hospitals in the first year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She died on 4 July 1934 at the Sancellemoz sanatorium in Passy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-savoie">Haute-Savoie</a>. When her body was exhumed in 1995, a French radiation-protection office concluded that she could not have been exposed to lethal levels of radium in life and suggested that radiography in the war was a likelier cause.</p><p>She and Pierre were moved to the Paris Panthéon in 1995, making her the first woman interred there on her own merits. She declined to patent the radium-isolation process so that others could research freely, and the element curium is named in her honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marie Curie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Isolating it took <b>Marie Curie</b> another twelve years.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As <b>Marie Curie</b> she pioneered research on radioactivity and became the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two sciences.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pierre-curie.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pierre Curie</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 1859–1906</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But it also needs dreamers, for whom the unselfish following of a purpose is so imperative that it becomes impossible for them to devote much attention to their own material benefit.&#8221;</p><footer>Pierre Curie (1923) &#183; tr. Charlotte and Vernon Kellogg, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/69617/pg69617-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yet it was in this miserable old shed that we passed the best and happiest years of our life, devoting our entire days to our work.&#8221;</p><footer>Pierre Curie (1923), autobiographical notes &#183; tr. Charlotte and Vernon Kellogg, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/69617/pg69617-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
